    "chunk_size_bytes": 1802,
    "chunk_text": "When the University of Ceylon was established, and later when the Universities Act of 1978 formalised the system, academic freedom and institutional autonomy were embraced as indispensable principles. For decades, autonomy protected universities from political turbulence, ensured that academic affairs remained internally regulated, and upheld quality standards. Yet by the late 20th century, research by Seneviratne (1999), Wickramasinghe (2014), and others had already begun documenting the gradual erosion of that independence due to politicised appointments, expanding bureaucratic controls, and shifting political priorities.\n\nEligibility widened to senior professors, professors, associate professors, and senior lecturers (Grade I).\n\nA dean elected from among heads of departments must relinquish that post.\n\nUniversity councils, bodies often dominated by political appointees, are\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0explicitly empowered to remove deans at any time.\n\nAppointed by the Council on the recommendation of the vice-chancellor.\n\nSelection must follow a rigid hierarchy: senior professor \u2192 professor \u2192 associate professor \u2192senior lecturer (Grade I/II).\n\nAppointees must be confirmed in their substantive posts and consent to appointment.\n\nIf no eligible academic exists, a candidate may be appointed from a related discipline.\n\nCouncils may remove heads of departments without specifying any grounds.\n\nTerm limits set at one term per department, with a second term only if no other eligible academic is available.\n\nIn effect, the most sensitive academic appointments are placed under bodies already shaped by political influence.\n\nThere is growing concern that the amendment may have been shaped by individuals with close political connections or personal interests in restructuring faculty level powers"
